's a fresh implementation, so I need to split that across multiple tasks since each can handle at most 4 requirements.\n\nI'm realizing I only have 4 task slots total. With fold needing 2 tasks and expand needing 4 tasks for all its requirements, I can't fit everything. Let me prioritize: fold gets 2 tasks, then I'll allocate the remaining 2 slots to the first batch of expand requirements (R1.1-R1.4), leaving the rest for later.\n\nSince R groups within each utility build on each other, I'll structure the fold tasks with R3 first, then R4 as a dependent task. For expand, I'll start with R1 as the foundation.\n\nNow I need to translate these into proper YAML task descriptions that the stitch agent can work with. I'll need to pull in the SRD requirements, specify which files to create or modify, and define clear acceptance criteria based on the specification documents. I'm realizing I should follow the recent commit message patterns as my model for task titles and structure. Looking at the nl implementation, the format is `[stitch] cmd/<name>: implement <srd-id> R<group>.1-R<group>.4`. I'll apply that same pattern here, making sure to use ASCII dashes and structure the YAML with proper id/text mappings for requirements, design decisions, and acceptance criteria. My plan is to create four tasks: one for fold R3.1-R3.4, another for fold R4.1-R4.4, and two for expand covering R1.1-R1.4 and R2.1-R2.4, keeping each description self-contained so the stitch agent can work independently. I'm laying out the remaining tasks for the stitch implementation—Task 2 creates the expand command from scratch following the srd024-expand spec, and Task 3 builds on that with additional requirements. Now I need to finalize the YAML structure, but I'm second-guessing whether the task dependencies and file modifications are correctly specified. I'm realizing the P2 rule about splitting implementation and tests conflicts with the practical pattern I see in recent commits—those tasks combine both in a single unit. Given the weight constraint and the established precedent, I'll stick with the pattern of including tests alongside implementation rather than creating separate tasks.\n\nFor the task title, I should follow the format from recent commits: include the [stitch] prefix, the command path, and the specific SRD requirements covered.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what to include in required_reading—the SRD file itself, any existing source files, the requirements.yaml to verify weights, and relevant pkg/ utilities for testing patterns. The CLAUDE.md will already be available to the stitch agent, so I don't need to list it explicitly.\n\nFor design decisions, I'm pulling from the project's established patterns: differential testing via pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests, SIGPIPE handling at main startup, g-prefixed reference binaries, LC_ALL=C in tests, and using exec.LookPath with test skipping for missing references.\n\nBefore finalizing, I need to verify the weight constraint—checking that the four sub-requirements (R4.1 through R4.4) each have weight 1, totaling 4 and staying within the limit. I'm verifying that each SRD requirement maps to exactly one task requirement, and I notice the constraint is satisfied across all the items I'm checking. Looking at how the previous nl tasks were structured, I see the pattern uses commit message format like \"implement srd022-nl R4.1-R4.4\" to reference the SRD and requirement ranges. For the task descriptions, I should write self-contained requirements that reference the SRD requirements by ID, telling the agent to implement them since they'll have access to the SRD itself.\n\nNow I'm laying out the task structure with the required reading materials, file modifications, and requirement mappings that will guide the agent through implementing the fold command requirements. I need to set up the SIGPIPE handler in main and configure the test environment with LC_ALL=C for consistency. The acceptance criteria confirm that all